"""
Walk-Forward Early Stopping — training halted by the future, not by a random split.

WHAT IT DOES: The gambler's problem — when do you stop training before your model
starts lying to you? A model that trains too long memorizes noise instead of signal.
This welds two tools: walk-forward validation (train on the past, test only on the
next slice of time it has never seen) and early stopping with patience (watch the
forward error; when it hasn't improved for N consecutive rounds, stop). The demo
shows train error sinking to 0.45 "memorizing noise" while forward error bottoms at
0.944 and then RISES — that rise is the lie beginning.

WHEN TO USE: Tuning training duration/rounds for any iterative model on time-ordered
data. Where you meet this: every neural network's EarlyStopping callback, every
trading-strategy backtest, every Kaggle submission that didn't overfit.

GUARANTEES: Training error undercuts forward error (overfit detected). Forward curve
is a U — continuing past the best round hurts (final > best × 1.05). Early stopping
fires before the end and exactly `patience` rounds after the forward minimum.

NOT: A general-purpose early stopper. The walk-forward split requires time-ordered
data. Random validation splits would leak future regime into training — the forward
window cannot.
"""
